  "market_commentary": {
    "overview": "Sale & Purchase: The second-hand market stayed busy, with forty-eight vessels reported sold across all \nsectors and values holding firm, particularly for prompt, charter-free tankers. \nIn the bulker sector, the 2007-built Setouchi-max CAPE ALLIANCE (206,190 dwt, Imabari) changed hands for \nregion USD 35 - 36.5 mill, against the USD 23.5 mill paid for the two-year-older CAPE MERLIN in December \n2025, showing how far values for older large tonnage have moved. The 2012-built Kamsarmax PANORAMA \n(81,504 dwt, Hyundai Samho) was sold for region USD 19.8 - 20.5 mill basis delivery in January 2027 with \nsurveys due, compared with USD 22.5 mill for her one-year-younger sister DAEBO GLADSTONE in September. \nBulgarian buyers paid USD 47 mill for the brand new, scrubber-fitted Ultramax DELMAX66 (66,565 dwt, 2026 \nTsuneishi Cebu). In the Supramax segment, clients of ADNOC acquired the Japanese-built BAHAMAS L (56,141 \ndwt, 2012 Mitsui) for USD 22.25 mill, while the CHARADE (58,109 dwt, 2013 Tsuneishi Cebu) fetched region \nUSD 22.5 - 22.8 mill. The scrubber-fitted MIAO ZHOU (58,595 dwt, 2013 Kawasaki) was sold for region excess \nUSD 23 mill and the SW MISTRAL I (58,110 dwt, 2011 Tsuneishi Zhoushan) for USD 20 mill. Among the \nHandysizes, Greek buyers paid USD 21.5 mill for the 2016-built ASTRO CASTOR (37,347 dwt, Avic), and the \nbox-shaped sisters PAIWAN WISDOM and DAIWAN WISDOM (2010 Hakodate) were sold en bloc for USD \n11.75 mill each. The OBE DINARES (33,440 dwt, 2008 Shin Kochi) achieved region USD 12.4 mill and the LUCKY \nVOYAGER (37,317 dwt, 2009 Saiki) is rumoured trading at region USD 14.5 mill. In total, 16 bulk carriers were \nreported sold: 2 Capesizes, 2 Kamsarmaxes, 1 Ultramax, 5 Supramaxes and 6 Handysizes. \nOn the tanker front, 25 sales were reported, with buying interest concentrated on product tankers, although \nthe VLCC sector also saw activity: the PHOENIX VIGOR (309,887 dwt, 2009 Imabari) is rumoured trading at \nregion USD 150 mill and the RISING SUN 1 (297,439 dwt, 2005 Dalian) is committed at region USD 126 mill. \nThe Suezmax FLEVES (158,517 dwt, 2012 Hyundai) achieved region USD 110 mill basis prompt delivery in the \nUAE, and Greek buyers paid USD 65 mill for the LR2 HAIMA (104,991 dwt, 2009 Mitsui). In the MR segment, \nfour Chengxi-built newbuildings, the CS FUJAIRAH, CS ROTTERDAM, CS HOUSTON and CS SINGAPORE, were \nreported at region USD 53 - 55 mill each, while the 2008 HMD-built MAYFAIR (49,999 dwt) fetched USD 27 \nmill, giving her sellers a profit of USD 10.5 mill on the USD 16.5 mill they paid in August 2025. In the smaller \nsizes, the MarineLINE-coated HELENE MAERSK and HENNING MAERSK (2010 STX Offshore) were sold en bloc \nfor USD 60 mill, while the SEYCHELLES PIONEER (37,500 dwt, 2005 Lindenau) achieved USD 14.2 mill. \nIn the container sector, five vessels changed hands. The Ice Class II SONGA PUMA (2,797 teu, 2009 Yangfan) \nwas sold for region USD 40 mill, Indian buyers acquired the OEL VARUN and OEL SURYA (2,478 teu, 2005 & \n2006 Aker) en bloc for USD 20.75 mill and USD 22.03 mill respectively, and the 2,029 teu Jiangsu Sentosa \nnewbuildings YANG LIAN HAI and HONG LAN GUO JI fetched USD 37.5 mill each. \nIn the gas sector, Navigare Capital acquired the VLGC NAVIGARE GAIA (82,441 cbm, 2015 Hyundai Ulsan) for \nUSD 96.5 mill against a two-year time charter to Shell, while the older GAS WARICH (82,026 cbm, 2008 DSME) \nfetched USD 81 mill. \nFor a detailed review and additional information regarding this week's transactions, kindly refer to our \n\"Representative Sales\" section.",
    "demolition": "Bangladesh buyers have raised their numbers noticeably to retain their position as the best \npayers in the region, as reflected by the 1994-built OCEANIC STAR (69,043 dwt) at region USD 540 - 545 per \nLT including bunkers, while candidates remain scarce with owners keeping older tonnage trading in the \nstrong freight markets. India remained calm, with the festive season weighing on local steel demand but \nspecialist units still attracting interest. Pakistan was quieter, with local plate prices falling and interest rates \nraised to 12%, while Turkey held steady on limited supply. \nFor an overview of demolition market rates, please check our comprehensive demometer below:"
  },
